---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import "../styles/global.css";

const posts = await getCollection("blog");
const sortedPosts = posts.sort((a, b) => b.data.day - a.data.day);
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Isaac Neibaur's 100-day dashboard project for building rapid-response data skills with Python, Astro, and Cloudflare."
    />
    <title>100daydash.blog</title>
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <main class="home-shell">
      <section class="home-hero">
        <p>100 dashboards in 100 days</p>
        <h1>100daydash.blog</h1>
        <p>
          Isaac Neibaur's public sprint to turn disciplined data engineering
          practice into useful dashboards, shipped one day at a time.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="post-list" aria-labelledby="about-project">
        <h2 id="about-project">About The Project</h2>
        <article>
          <p>Who</p>
          <h3>
            <a href="https://isaacneibaur.com">Isaac Neibaur</a>
          </h3>
          <p>
            Isaac Neibaur is a Data Engineer & Platform Architect using this
            project as a public practice field for disciplined, production-aware
            dashboard work.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p>Challenge</p>
          <h3>Build 100 dashboards in 100 days</h3>
          <p>
            The challenge is designed to develop consistency, rapid-response
            data skills, and practical data storytelling by repeatedly moving
            from question to real dataset, analysis, visualization, and
            published dashboard.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p>Tech</p>
          <h3>Python + Astro + Cloudflare monorepo</h3>
          <p>
            The stack stays intentionally local-first and reviewable while
            supporting reusable Python dashboard logic, Markdown publishing, and
            static web delivery.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p>Tooling Range</p>
          <h3>Broad dashboarding practice over time</h3>
          <p>
            The project will intentionally explore a wide range of dashboarding
            tools, platforms, and standards, including Power BI, Tableau,
            Cognos, Oracle, Google Analytics, Azure, AWS, Supabase, Google
            Sheets, Excel/CSV, and real public APIs where low/no-cost options
            are practical.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p>Data Standard</p>
          <h3>Real data whenever possible</h3>
          <p>
            Dashboards should use real data rather than mock data whenever
            possible, with source context, limitations, and assumptions made
            visible enough for readers to understand what the dashboard can and
            cannot claim.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p>Engineering Rigor</p>
          <h3>CI/CD, validation, security, and coverage</h3>
          <p>
            Each meaningful change is expected to stay compatible with CI/CD,
            metadata validation, security checks, and the project coverage gate
            of at least 80% for modified reusable logic.
          </p>
        </article>
      </section>

      <section class="post-list" aria-labelledby="latest-posts">
        <h2 id="latest-posts">Latest Posts</h2>
        {
          sortedPosts.map((post) => (
            <article>
              <p>Day {post.data.day}</p>
              <h3>
                <a href={`/blog/${post.id}/`}>{post.data.title}</a>
              </h3>
              <p>{post.data.description}</p>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </section>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
